From: Vipul Cariappa <vipulcariappa@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 09:09:57 +0530
Subject: Stop a magic's argument from swallowing the cell's first line

The cell-magic split spelled the optional argument after the magic name as
`(?:\s.*)?`. In ECMAScript `.` excludes newlines but `\s` includes them, so on

    %%python
    import libcppjit
    print(libcppjit)

`\s` matched the newline after "python" and `.*` went on to take the whole next
line: the magic received "python\nimport libcppjit" as its argument line and a
body starting at `print`. The import silently never ran, and the cell failed
with a NameError pointing at what looked like its first line.

`%%python` in the demo notebook only worked because a blank line follows the
magic -- that blank line was what got eaten. Restrict the class to spaces and
tabs, which is all an argument on that line can be made of. The line-magic
pattern had the same shape and the same problem.

(cherry picked from commit a3234d98ad480bbfc0b92a72b310233dc99c5f08)
---
 include/xeus-cpp/xmanager.hpp | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/xeus-cpp/xmanager.hpp b/include/xeus-cpp/xmanager.hpp
index 3292ce3..6bee87d 100644
--- a/include/xeus-cpp/xmanager.hpp
+++ b/include/xeus-cpp/xmanager.hpp
@@ -156,7 +156,9 @@ namespace xcpp
                     kernel_res["traceback"] = nl::json::array();
                     return;
                 }
-                std::regex re_magic_cell(R"(^\%{2}(\w+(?:\s.*)?)\n((?:.*\n?)*))");
+                // [ \t] rather than \s: \s matches a newline, which let the
+                // magic's own line swallow the first line of the cell body.
+                std::regex re_magic_cell(R"(^\%{2}(\w+(?:[ \t].*)?)\n((?:.*\n?)*))");
                 std::smatch split_code;
                 std::regex_search(code, split_code, re_magic_cell);
                 apply(magic_name[1], split_code[1], split_code[2]);
@@ -177,7 +179,9 @@ namespace xcpp
                     kernel_res["traceback"] = {};
                     return;
                 }
-                std::regex re_magic_line(R"(^\%(\w+(?:\s.*)?))");
+                // See above: \s here would pull the next line into the
+                // argument of a line magic.
+                std::regex re_magic_line(R"(^\%(\w+(?:[ \t].*)?))");
                 std::smatch split_code;
                 std::regex_search(code, split_code, re_magic_line);
                 apply(magic_name[1], split_code[1]);
